A 2x icns chunk is not an icon of twice the size.

SWIFT PUBLISHER OPENS A TEMPLATE END TO END because of this. Its trial licence dialog was 1316x1104
on a 1256x684 output, placed at -30,-397 so that two edges hung off the screen, and the click that
dismisses it could never land. It is now 804x592 at 226,115, fully on screen, and clicking Try It
dismisses it and leaves the document on screen with its rulers, page thumbnails, zoom control and
inspector. LOOKED AT, both before and after.

THE CHAIN, measured end to end and not guessed:
  The dialog is built IN CODE, not from a nib: its text lives in Resources/*.lproj/license.strings
  and in the executable, and license2.nib, which I first blamed, has neither watermark nor Try It.
  Its NSImageView was 1024x1024, and CIDER_TRACE_SVFRAME=NSImageView logged twelve frame changes in
  the whole run with not one of them 1024, so the view was BORN that size. The only thing that
  sizes a view to an image is +[NSImageView imageViewWithImage:], which is correct: Apple documents
  it as returning a view sized to fit. So the size came from the IMAGE.

  -[NSImage size] with no explicit size answers the LARGEST representation, and AppIcon.icns
  carries ic10, which is 1024x1024 PIXELS. But ic10 is 512 POINTS AT 2x. ic11 through ic14 are the
  same: their pixel dimensions are double their size in points. An NSImageRep built here carries
  only pixels, so every 2x chunk entered the image claiming twice its real size, and the app icon
  measured 1024x1024 instead of 512x512. Anything sized from an application icon was eight times
  too big.

THE FIX. The icns reader dispatches on the payload MAGIC, which is right, and therefore never knew
the type; it now also reads the type tag to recognise the five 2x chunks. They are HELD BACK rather
than dropped, and used only if no 1x chunk decoded at all, so an icns that carries only 2x variants
still has an icon. Every icns built by iconutil carries the 1x twin of each 2x chunk, so nothing is
lost in practice: the point sizes 16, 32, 128, 256 and 512 all remain.

This is not specific to one application. Any code path that asks an application icon how big it is
was getting the wrong answer.

Task #246.
